  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 222 AM CST Thu Nov 5 2026\n\nThrough mid next week (Wednesday)...\n\nThis morning into this weekend: Early this morning, GOES East \nwater vapor and RAP analysis indicate cold core upper low over the\nGreat Lakes and another over the Rockies. At the surface, a cold \nfront moved through the area, with surface high pressure building \ninto the ArkLaTex. This pattern will continue to filter in cooler,\ndrier air in the wake. Highs will continue to be seasonably warm \ntoday and Friday (70F to 82F today and Friday), with much more \nseasonable and cooler lows (45F to 54F). With cooler air sliding \ndown into the Delta region, dense fog will continue to be a \nconcern where cooler air has swept in over wet ground. REFS probs \nkeep most concerns along and northwest of the Natchez Trace \nParkway while recent trends in gridded LAMP and HRRR and \nobservations indicate potential in wetter and more recent rain \nareas along the Interstate 59 corridor. Based on these trends, \nexpanded the Dense Fog Advisory east from Simpson, Smith, Jasper \nand Clarke and the Elevated for dense fog to include Lincoln, \nLawrence and then the newly added fog headline areas from Simpson \nover to Clarke. Leaned on the REFS probs for Lincoln into Lawrence\nto add the Elevated there. Expanded the Limited to all of the \nrest of the area for patchy dense fog and will continue to monitor\nthe need for any additional future adjustments to fog \nheadlines/HWO graphic. Fog potential will be similar again tonight\ninto Friday morning, as crossover temps will be extremely \nfavorable for dense fog (8F to 10F). Based on that, added a \nLimited in similar areas REFS probs indicated this morning but may\nneed additional adjustments.\n\nAs the upper trough phases over the Great Plains into the upper\nlow, it will swing east into the weekend, with subtle shortwave\nridging moving in by late weekend. This will be in advance of an\nupper low over the Rio Grande Valley into central Mexico, with\nincreased isentropic ascent and clouds into the weekend. 1025mb\nsurface high will build in late weekend into the Mid South,\nbringing much cooler temperatures (low to upper 60s north of\nInterstate 20 Saturday and Sunday, while low 70s Saturday and mid\nto upper 60s Sunday). Seasonably cool lows, some 5F to 10F below,\nwill be the norm (39F to 50F Friday night and 35F to 42F Saturday\nand Sunday nights). Patchy frost may be a concern across the \nregion Saturday and Sunday nights. There will be some gradient\nwind Saturday night and more calm Sunday night, but winds should\nremain light enough to support some frost potential, especially in\nlow-lying and sheltered areas. Now is a good time to ensure \nplants, pets, and people are considered with more routine lows\nnearing frost to light freeze potential. Considering adding a \nLimited graphic in HWO for frost during these nights, mainly in \nnorth to northeast MS.\n\nEarly to mid next week: Quiet, partly cloudy and mostly dry\npattern will persist into the following week. Upper low parked\nover west-central TX will eject eastward, keeping moist ascent\nacross the Gulf Coast states. Some clouds will persist during that\ntime and cannot rule out isolated rain shower during this time.\nWith some thermal temps warming, highs will moderate from\nseasonable early week to seasonably warm mid week (65F to 75F\nMonday to 70F to 78F by Wednesday). Lows will remain seasonably\ncool but gradually less cold. /DC/",
